  "content": {
    "id": "p33-question87-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Solve $1+\\log_2(e^{2x}-1)=\\log_2(e^x+1)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=\\ln\\left(\\frac32\\right)$.\n\nUse the quotient law and $e^{2x}-1=(e^x-1)(e^x+1)$ on the domain $x>0$.",
      "worked": "The argument $e^{2x}-1$ must be positive, so $x>0$. The other argument is always positive. Move the logarithmic term and use the quotient law.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{e^x+1}{e^{2x}-1}\\right)\\\\1&=\\log_2\\left(\\frac1{e^x-1}\\right)\\\\\\frac1{e^x-1}&=2\\\\e^x-1&=\\frac12\\\\e^x&=\\frac32\\\\x&=\\ln\\left(\\frac32\\right)\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe solution is positive, so it satisfies the original domain.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 82
  },
